1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:04,925
Welcome to Exchange Korean Rules, version two point three point one.

2
00:00:05,145 --> 00:00:13,399
The public kit now includes both verified rule files. You
do not need to download SQL media just to start using it.

3
00:00:13,619 --> 00:00:24,028
Get inspects servers. Set applies the guarded workaround. Install verifies
the bundled files or prepares a replacement source when explicitly requested.

4
00:00:24,248 --> 00:00:31,263
This is custom automation of published guidance, not
a Microsoft-signed hotfix or a permanent product fix.

5
00:00:31,483 --> 00:00:37,241
The examples are illustrative. This recording
does not apply changes to an Exchange server.

6
00:00:38,438 --> 00:00:45,977
Open the current guide on GitHub main and choose the two
point three point one zip, not an older source-only archive.

7
00:00:46,197 --> 00:00:50,418
Use your browser's Show in folder option to locate the download.

8
00:00:50,638 --> 00:00:57,164
After verifying its source and checksum, extract
the complete kit into a dedicated working folder.

9
00:00:57,384 --> 00:01:02,794
Keep the module, manifest, private folder,
and payload beside the entry-point scripts.

10
00:01:03,014 --> 00:01:11,384
The example uses drive D, but you choose the location. Normal
use does not create another copy of the kit under drive C.

11
00:01:12,605 --> 00:01:16,517
Windows may mark a downloaded zip as coming from the Internet.

12
00:01:16,737 --> 00:01:22,730
First review the approved source and compare the
calculated checksum with the published release value.

13
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:28,419
Only after you trust the package, unblock that exact zip and extract a fresh copy.

14
00:01:28,639 --> 00:01:32,316
Calculating a hash is not the same as checking that it matches.

15
00:01:32,536 --> 00:01:40,436
This procedure does not disable PowerShell policy, application
control, or your organization's security requirements.

16
00:01:41,647 --> 00:01:49,047
Unblocking the zip after extraction does not remove
Internet-zone tags from files that were already extracted.

17
00:01:49,267 --> 00:01:55,772
After reviewing the kit, you can unblock its script and
module files together, including the private folder.

18
00:01:55,992 --> 00:02:01,440
Use the dedicated kit folder only, not the whole software or temporary directory.

19
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:13,113
Get Execution Policy List is a read-only diagnostic. A signing requirement or
application-control restriction needs the appropriate organizational process.

20
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:21,885
The administrator consent window is UAC, not an Internet-zone
warning. Unblocking files does not remove that requirement.

21
00:02:23,105 --> 00:02:29,406
In a complete bundled kit, running Install without
arguments only verifies the two rule files.

22
00:02:29,626 --> 00:02:37,442
That verification is read-only and does not require
administrator access, download media, or create directories.

23
00:02:37,662 --> 00:02:44,284
You may skip this optional step and use Get and
Set directly. Set performs its own required checks.

24
00:02:44,504 --> 00:02:54,068
Set looks for payload beside its own kit, even when called from a different
working directory. An explicit alternate payload path still takes precedence.

25
00:02:54,288 --> 00:03:03,395
An invalid or partial payload fails clearly. Only a custom or
historical kit with no payload shows preparation guidance instead.

26
00:03:04,605 --> 00:03:11,835
Run Get to inspect the observed installation. Get does not
need the rule payload and does not apply the workaround.

27
00:03:12,055 --> 00:03:19,328
The tool requires its exact supported Exchange
build and DLL identity, with both rule files absent.

28
00:03:19,548 --> 00:03:29,177
A server called a client-access or relay server is not excluded just by
that label. Check the actual installed components and supported build.

29
00:03:29,397 --> 00:03:40,339
Modern Exchange client-access services are part of the Mailbox role. Dedicated
Edge and older CAS-only installations are not validated by this walkthrough.

30
00:03:40,559 --> 00:03:47,904
Eligible means the file checks passed. It is not proof
of a deadlock, a completed restart, or recovered search.

31
00:03:49,105 --> 00:03:55,768
An incompatible target shows the found and
required build and DLL version on separate lines.

32
00:03:55,988 --> 00:04:05,560
The console no longer prints the long size and hash explanation here, but
those verification checks still run and the detailed reports retain the

33
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:06,352
information.

34
00:04:06,572 --> 00:04:11,754
If only size or hash differs, the console directs you to the detailed report.

35
00:04:11,974 --> 00:04:19,854
Existing rules and incompatible installations are yellow
skips. Set leaves them unchanged and continues the list.

36
00:04:20,074 --> 00:04:30,590
Investigate a partial rule pair rather than overwriting it. Routine skips do
not automatically require Support; real operation failures still halt rollout.

37
00:04:31,772 --> 00:04:36,900
Remote Set uses the verified payload on the computer where you launch the command.

38
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:45,192
It transfers the small runtime, inspects the remote server,
and sends the two rule files only when that target is eligible.

39
00:04:45,412 --> 00:04:51,672
The remote operation verifies them again before
copying them into Exchange's Native directory.

40
00:04:51,892 --> 00:05:01,990
The two files total about seven hundred fifty-six kibibytes. The large
SQL package is neither copied nor extracted on each remote server.

41
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:10,814
Use existing authorized PowerShell remoting. Do not weaken
authentication or trust configuration to make a connection work.

42
00:05:12,022 --> 00:05:19,560
Computer names can be the first positional argument.
A CSV still uses the explicit CSV Path parameter.

43
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:27,811
From an Exchange management shell, export the native Name and
FQDN properties without inventing a Computer Name property.

44
00:05:28,031 --> 00:05:34,311
The importer selects Computer Name, otherwise
FQDN, otherwise Name, once for the whole file.

45
00:05:34,531 --> 00:05:43,159
A blank or invalid selected value stops the run instead of
silently choosing another column. Duplicate targets are rejected.

46
00:05:43,379 --> 00:05:53,254
Get validates the list before contact. Its remote inspection can create
code staging and reports, but does not copy rule files into Exchange.

47
00:05:54,438 --> 00:06:02,701
One to three targets show detailed state, action, and next
steps. Four or more use compact status totals and report paths.

48
00:06:02,921 --> 00:06:12,518
That display change does not discard any target. Dollar report contains
typed rows, and dollar report Files contains the export locations.

49
00:06:12,738 --> 00:06:19,571
Use Pass Through when you want explicit pipeline
assignment, or JSON for a separate automation process.

50
00:06:19,791 --> 00:06:28,342
The script's own UAC handoff returns its results, but an unrelated
PowerShell process cannot set variables in its parent shell.

51
00:06:28,562 --> 00:06:33,787
Errors and required recovery prompts remain visible even when the list is large.

52
00:06:34,980 --> 00:06:43,341
Normal runs write CSV, detailed JSON, and final JSON
Lines into a unique report directory on the caller.

53
00:06:43,561 --> 00:06:47,475
The existing report-root name is retained for compatibility.

54
00:06:47,695 --> 00:06:55,319
For Splunk file monitoring, collect the final JSON Lines
file rather than the repeatedly rewritten rollout checkpoint.

55
00:06:55,539 --> 00:07:03,515
Use the documented extraction model. Do not combine search-time
and indexed JSON extraction for the same source type.

56
00:07:03,735 --> 00:07:14,133
The customer's administrator still needs to verify event counts, timestamps,
and duplicates. The tool does not configure or validate their Splunk pipeline.

57
00:07:15,313 --> 00:07:19,386
Set defaults to Apply, so start with What If before making a change.

58
00:07:19,606 --> 00:07:25,385
The adjacent payload is automatic, and the
maintenance-window switch is no longer required.

59
00:07:25,605 --> 00:07:34,669
Local preview can inspect local state. Remote preview makes no
remote connections, so it is not a connectivity or eligibility test.

60
00:07:34,889 --> 00:07:39,142
Use remote Get for actual inspection before the modifying run.

61
00:07:39,362 --> 00:07:48,901
At the normal confirmation preference, Confirm is optional. What If,
UAC consent, and per-server recovery attestation are different controls.

62
00:07:50,105 --> 00:07:54,829
After the health checks and operational approval, start with one pilot server.

63
00:07:55,049 --> 00:08:04,348
Restart Search is still an explicit request. The old maintenance-window
switch is accepted for compatibility but no longer gates the operation.

64
00:08:04,568 --> 00:08:10,423
Without Restart Search, Set stages the files
and reports that a restart is still required.

65
00:08:10,643 --> 00:08:18,832
The requested restart affects Host Controller and content
processing. It does not automatically restart Transport or Store.

66
00:08:19,052 --> 00:08:29,503
Plan for impact on search and affected processing; do not promise zero
mail-delivery impact. A stable process does not prove that the workload recovered.

67
00:08:30,688 --> 00:08:36,212
For mailbox search checks, use a mailbox whose
active database is on the changed server.

68
00:08:36,432 --> 00:08:44,184
Verify ordinary and Korean messages deliver and become
searchable, using unique terms and a negative control.

69
00:08:44,404 --> 00:08:54,841
For an Exchange relay workload, also validate the actual relay route and affected
processing. Do not invent a mailbox-search result for a server that cannot

70
00:08:54,841 --> 00:08:55,644
provide one.

71
00:08:55,864 --> 00:09:01,962
Review fresh errors and queues, and treat historical
indexing backlog as a separate observation.

72
00:09:02,182 --> 00:09:11,022
If a check fails, stop rollout and preserve evidence. Investigate
the actual caller before any separately approved recovery action.

73
00:09:12,230 --> 00:09:17,773
After the pilot passes, expand with a reviewed list and an explicit restart request.

74
00:09:17,993 --> 00:09:25,981
Restarted remote rollout is serial. Validate the current
server before entering the exact recovered statement requested.

75
00:09:26,201 --> 00:09:31,564
Do not enter that statement merely because the
files are present or the service is running.

76
00:09:31,784 --> 00:09:41,007
Expected skips continue without modification, but a true operation
or recovery failure stops the run and leaves later targets Not Run.

77
00:09:41,227 --> 00:09:47,157
Four or more targets may have compact output, but
the recovery gate is still visible and required.

78
00:09:48,355 --> 00:09:59,108
For preparation, use elevated sixty-four-bit Windows PowerShell five point
one. Download is an explicit fallback for fresh media extraction or a kit that

79
00:09:59,108 --> 00:10:01,005
needs its payload prepared.

80
00:10:01,225 --> 00:10:08,667
You can instead supply an existing SQL executable,
or a folder containing the two verified rule files.

81
00:10:08,887 --> 00:10:16,521
SQL Server is not installed. The fallback only
extracts media locally and verifies the required files.

82
00:10:16,741 --> 00:10:23,405
The large media download is not repeated by Get or
Set, and it is never needed on every remote target.

83
00:10:23,625 --> 00:10:32,252
A partial download or identity conflict is an error, not permission
to bypass checks or overwrite an invalid existing payload.

84
00:10:33,438 --> 00:10:42,011
Normal preparation now fills only the current kit's adjacent
payload. It does not generate another working copy or zip.

85
00:10:42,231 --> 00:10:49,514
From an elevated preparation session, request a new Output
Directory only when you want a separate portable package.

86
00:10:49,734 --> 00:10:59,077
Without that request, the package and zip-hash return values are empty
because no zip was created. The payload path points to the current kit.

87
00:10:59,297 --> 00:11:04,340
Using verified BINs does not create an empty extraction directory.

88
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:15,812
Actual SQL-media extraction still uses a unique work folder for safe extraction
and diagnostics. Existing user folders are not automatically deleted.

89
00:11:17,022 --> 00:11:24,957
Zero may mean eligible detection, a preview, or a completed
operation; it is not proof that the workload recovered.

90
00:11:25,177 --> 00:11:31,063
One is an error. Ten means a Search restart is still required, not a Windows reboot.

91
00:11:31,283 --> 00:11:42,138
Twenty indicates an incompatible installation or existing rules that need
review. A mixed file-only run that actually stages rules can still return ten.

92
00:11:42,358 --> 00:11:48,596
Preserve the reports and operation receipts.
Partial changes are not automatically rolled back.

93
00:11:48,816 --> 00:11:59,405
Use Support for uncertain partial recovery or exceptional rollback, not every
routine skip. Rollback remains local, receipt-bound, and dependent on actual

94
00:11:59,405 --> 00:12:01,261
Microsoft Support approval.

95
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:09,272
The two rule files are now in GitHub and in the public
zip, so the normal workflow starts with the complete kit.

96
00:12:09,492 --> 00:12:16,775
No per-server SQL preparation, manual payload handoff,
or mandatory maintenance-window switch is needed.

97
00:12:16,995 --> 00:12:21,153
Restarts remain explicit, and operational planning still matters.

98
00:12:21,373 --> 00:12:28,102
Unblocking a trusted package is not permission
to bypass UAC or organizational policy.

99
00:12:28,322 --> 00:12:35,007
Use the current guide and reviewed target list, then
base rollout decisions on actual workload evidence.

100
00:12:36,188 --> 00:12:46,637
The two point three point one release passed three hundred forty-four runtime
tests, the same number against the extracted kit, and five repository layout

101
00:12:46,637 --> 00:12:47,044
tests.

102
00:12:47,264 --> 00:12:54,343
The published files were downloaded back from GitHub and
checked, including native verification of the bundled payload.

103
00:12:54,563 --> 00:13:01,526
Those checks do not certify customer workload recovery,
production load, or historical indexing backlog.

104
00:13:01,746 --> 00:13:07,962
This walkthrough updates the commands and explanation;
it is not a recording of a fresh remediation.

105
00:13:08,182 --> 00:13:17,447
Use the current guide, inspect your targets, preview the change, and
expand only after a deliberately chosen pilot passes the workload checks.
